# ============================ JLib ============================
# Everything the build produces lives in exactly one place, so the ignore list is short.
# See BUILD.md: OutDir/IntDir are set once in JLib.Common.props and point at build\.

# All build output, for every project and every configuration.
/build/

# CMake. The generated tree, plus the files CMake drops beside the source when someone configures
# in-place or uses the VS "Open Folder" CMake integration.
/cmake-build*/
/out/
CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
CMakeUserPresets.json
cmake_install.cmake
*.cmake.stamp

# Visual Studio's per-user solution state (IntelliSense database, window layout, breakpoints).
.vs/

# Per-user project settings: local debugger arguments, working directory, which machine you
# deployed to. Never the same for two people, and never worth a merge conflict.
*.user
*.suo
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates

# Stray per-project output, in case a project is ever built outside the umbrella (a bare
# msbuild on a .vcxproj with no SolutionDir does exactly this - see the BUILD GOTCHA in BUILD.md
# about $(SolutionDir) being empty, which lands output somewhere unexpected).
x64/
Win32/
[Aa][Rr][Mm]64/

# ...but those patterns are UNANCHORED and git's ignore matching is CASE-INSENSITIVE on Windows, so
# `Win32/` above also matches the SOURCE directories src/win32/ and tests/win32/. That failure is
# silent and nasty: Scheduler/src/win32/ContextSwitch.asm only survives because it was tracked
# before the rule existed, and a NEW file added there is invisible to `git add -A` -- which is
# exactly what happened while syncing the Windows-on-ARM64 port (2026-08-17). The library would then
# be pushed referencing a context switch that is not in the repository.
# Fixed GENERALLY rather than one directory at a time, because the hazard is not specific to win32:
# `[Aa][Rr][Mm]64/` would swallow a future src/arm64/ exactly the same way, and the only reason it
# does not today is that the AArch64 source directories happen to be named "aarch64". Naming a
# directory after a platform is a completely reasonable thing for the next person to do.
#
# No source tree ever contains build output, so re-include src/ and tests/ wholesale. The
# directories themselves have to be re-included, not just their contents -- git cannot re-include a
# file whose parent directory is excluded, which is why `!*/src/**` alone would not be enough.
#
# ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. These sit BEFORE the artifact patterns below (*.tlog, *.pdb, *.iobj), so if
# anyone ever builds in-tree, those later patterns still win for the artifacts themselves -- last
# matching pattern decides. Do not move this block down.
!*/src/
!*/src/**
!*/tests/
!*/tests/**

# MSBuild incremental-build bookkeeping and linker leftovers.
*.tlog
*.ilk
*.exp
*.idb
*.iobj
*.ipdb
*.pdb
*.log
*.lastbuildstate
*.recipe

# NuGet, if a project ever reintroduces it. (The DirectX headers came from a package originally;
# they are vendored under ThirdParty/ now precisely so no restore step is needed.)
packages/
*.nupkg

# OS / editor noise.
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.vscode/


# ==================== DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED ====================
#
# There is no `*.lib` rule, and no bare `Debug/` or `Release/` rule. That is not an oversight:
#
#   ThirdParty/DirectXTex/lib/debug/DirectXTex.lib
#   ThirdParty/DirectXTex/lib/release/DirectXTex.lib
#
# are VENDORED binaries that must be committed - DirectXTex has no local source tree, so a fresh
# clone cannot link without them. A `Release/` rule would swallow that directory (git's ignore
# matching is case-insensitive on Windows, so it catches `release/` too), and a negation such as
# `!ThirdParty/**` CANNOT rescue it: git will not re-include a file whose parent directory is
# excluded. Build output is caught by the `/build/` rule above instead, which is precise and has
# no such collision.
#
# If you ever add a `*.lib` or `Release/` rule, run this first and expect two paths back:
#   git check-ignore -v ThirdParty/DirectXTex/lib/release/DirectXTex.lib
# Silence means it is about to be dropped from the repo.
